  "thread": {
    "kind": "question",
    "title": "Is the Ridgeline shell warm enough for winter hiking?",
    "aboutUrl": "https://www.example.com/jackets/ridgeline-rain-shell/#product-group",
    "opening": {
      "id": "5101",
      "text": "Planning day hikes in the White Mountains in January. Is the Ridgeline shell enough on its own, or what should I layer under it?",
      "author": {
        "name": "jkrause",
        "profileUrl": "https://www.example.com/community/members/jkrause/"
      },
      "date": "2026-09-30T19:12:00-04:00",
      "upvotes": 14
    },
    "replies": [
      {
        "id": "5102",
        "text": "On its own, no. It is a shell, so it blocks wind and rain but has no insulation. I wear a merino base layer and a light fleece under it and stay warm down to about 10F while moving.",
        "author": {
          "name": "Maya Okafor",
          "profileUrl": "https://www.example.com/community/members/maya-okafor/"
        },
        "date": "2026-09-30T20:05:00-04:00",
        "upvotes": 31,
        "accepted": true
      },
      {
        "id": "5103",
        "text": "Add a puffy for breaks. You cool down fast the moment you stop, and the shell traps the warmth well over a down layer.",
        "author": {
          "name": "trailmix",
          "profileUrl": "https://www.example.com/community/members/trailmix/"
        },
        "date": "2026-10-01T07:40:00-04:00",
        "upvotes": 9
      },
